P-280 step 2: name which descriptor an object carries, from the evidence

The join P-280 keys on, and the one binding that cannot live in the
judgement.

Once the classification is keyed per descriptor rather than per object,
an object reaches its permissions through a descriptor identity. If the
judgement were allowed to record that identity, a store saying "X uses
D2" where the evidence says "X uses D1" would validate every fingerprint
it holds and quietly attribute D2's permissions to X. A fingerprint
cannot stop that, because whoever edited the judgement recomputes it.

So ObjectPermissions.DescriptorId is read out of object_security_descriptor
under the evidence seal and the judgement is never asked. It is not part
of the descriptor's content and nothing is judged from it: it is the
join, stated by the only party entitled to state it. Null for an object
with no stored descriptor - 280,871 of the production capture's 940,067 -
which is not the same as an object that grants nothing, and is why
Availability exists beside it.

Four checks, each with its opposite:

  AnObjectWithAStoredDescriptorNamesIt
  TwoObjectsSharingADescriptorNameTheSameOne   - the join itself
  ObjectsCarryingDifferentDescriptorsNameDifferentOnes - without this, a
      build returning a constant passes the one above and every object in
      the forest reads back the first descriptor's permissions
  AnObjectWithNoStoredDescriptorNamesNone

The third asserts the stored descriptor count beside the two identities.
A bare NotEqual can only say "they matched" and leaves the cause to
guesswork - writer deduplicating two distinct descriptors into one, or
reader reporting one identity for two rows. Stating both halves names
the guilty one immediately. It was written that way because guessing
cost most of a session: the test appeared to fail with both ids reading
1, which looked like an evidence-reader defect serious enough to stop
the work. It was mutation residue. A `DescriptorId = 1` probe had been
restored in the source but the test ran --no-build against the assembly
still holding it. Comparing file timestamps did not catch it; mutating
to +100 and rebuilding produced 101 and 102 in one run. The standing
instruction to rebuild explicitly around a mutation exists for exactly
this, and skipping it manufactured a product defect that was never there.

Mutation-tested with explicit rebuilds before and after restoring.
2,982 Evidence and Judgement tests green.
